program            = 004_qualifiers
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = const, volatile and restrict on objects and parameters, with a truthful non-aliasing restrict contract
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
RESTRICT NON-ALIASING ARGUMENT. This is the most important undefined-behaviour risk in this
program, because a lying restrict is diagnosed by no warning flag and detected by neither
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer nor AddressSanitizer, so it would pass every automated gate while
silently invalidating every verdict the program produces. The guarantee here is structural
rather than incidental. scale_into declares int *restrict dst and const int *restrict src.
There is exactly one call site in the whole program, and it passes dest_data and
source_data. Those are two separately declared file-scope arrays of four ints each: dest_data
is a distinct object from source_data, neither is a pointer into the other, and their storage
does not overlap in any way. During the call, dst therefore accesses only dest_data and src
accesses only source_data, so no object modified through dst is ever read through src and the
restrict contract holds absolutely. Additionally src is const-qualified, so the source array
is not written at all. dest_data is written before it is read: scale_into fills all four
elements, and only then does the summing loop read them.

REMAINING UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. Every index is in range: the loop bound n is 4 and both arrays
have exactly four elements, so dst[i] and src[i] are valid for i = 0 through 3, and the
summing loop uses the same fixed bound of 4. No pointer is formed one past the end and
dereferenced. const_ptr_to_int is an int *const initialized to the address of the automatic
object target, so writing through it is well defined -- the pointer is immutable but the
pointee is a non-const int. ptr_to_const points at the genuinely const object const_source
and is only ever read through, never written through, so no const object is modified. Every
object read is initialized first: ticks, target, sum and i all have initializers or are
assigned before use, and dest_data has static storage duration so it is zero initialized even
before scale_into fills it. All arithmetic is on small int values far from the int range
edges, so there is no signed overflow: the largest intermediate value is 30. No shift is
performed. No object is modified twice between sequence points, and no printf call receives a
side-effecting argument at all. There is no aliasing violation anywhere: every object is
accessed only through an lvalue of its own declared type. No padding byte is read.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This program prints no size and no alignment at all, so the measured i686 width differences
(4-byte long and pointer versus 8 bytes on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64) cannot surface. All
printed values are small int quantities. No address or pointer value is printed: pointer
facts appear only as dereferenced int values. Plain char is not used, so the measured char
signedness difference (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is
irrelevant. The only printf conversion specifier used in the program is %d.

The volatile object ticks is read and written three times in sequence, by 1 then 2 then 3, and
the printed total is 6. What that single printed value can and cannot prove is worth stating
exactly, because overclaiming it would make the assertion look stronger than it is.

It CAN prove that the three read-modify-write operations were composed correctly: any backend
that dropped one of them, applied one twice, mis-ordered the reads relative to the writes, or
loaded a stale value would print something other than 6, and every such difference is caught by
oracle (a) against the reference compiler, by oracle (b) across the four backends, and by
oracle (c) against this record, at all three optimization levels. It also proves the value is
stable ACROSS optimization levels, since the same 6 is expected at -O0, -O1 and -O2.

It CANNOT prove that three distinct volatile accesses were actually emitted. A compiler that
illegally collapsed the sequence into one store of 6 would print 6 as well, so this output does
not distinguish the two. Whether the accesses survive individually is an instruction-level
property, and verifying it needs a white-box check on the generated code -- which is deliberately
outside this suite's oracles, all three of which compare only observable program behaviour. That
check belongs to the repository's existing per-backend code-generation tests, which inspect
emitted instructions directly; the note here is confined to what stdout can settle.

For a side effect whose omission IS observable from stdout, see the short-circuit programs in
area 06, where the assertion is that a counter did not change -- an omission the printed value
reports directly.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
const_read=42
volatile_seq=6
restrict_scaled=3 6 9 12 sum=30
const_qualified_ptr_target=42
const_pointer_write=19
END
